speechtext:
I am not making the
least complaint. I did not write the letter with any idea of secrecy at all, and I stand by the terms of that letter to the last period. When the late government was in office the Western Canada Colonization Association, as it was then called, was formed. It was formed on the basis of a voluntary association to assist in the settlement of near railway western

Supply-Immigration
lands, supported by the voluntary contribution of those who desired those lands settled, and particularly by those who had a special interest in that settlement. The late government made no proposal, nor did it receive favourably any suggestion, looking to exerting its own immigration activities through the instrumentality of the organization. The late government merely looked with favour upon the formation of a voluntary non-profit sharing organization. That position we took then, that position I reiterated without hesitation in the letter that was read; but just as we looked with favour upon the organization of a nonprofit sharing company for this work, just as we took that view of it we took, as well, the view that there should not be any system of assistance on the part of the administration, nor any system of inter-responsibility as between the Immigration department, the government of Canada and any land selling organization. That responsibility we declined to take; and my letter is not only entirely consistent with that position but emphasizes it from beginning to end. I do not favour any assistance by the government;
I do not favour any inter-locking responsibility as between the government of Canada and any land organization whatsoever;
I did not favour it at the time; I do not favour it now. That is the purport of the letter I wrote, and if the government feels that it can rest on any letter of mine as endorsing the policy that now is presented to this House, I am afraid it rests on a broken reed.
